    "author": "@emrickazor2610",
    "text": "I had to come here after seeing a video about the Picsmics comment string. While I do feel leaving my own comment tarnishes a strange and interesting piece of internet history, I also feel like I wanna leave my mark here and say a few things.\n\nFirst off, Drazen, you're incredible on the drums. I have been trying since before this video was uploaded to play the drums on RB, and I STILL cannot do it. I don't know why I can't, I am typically really good at keeping my rhythm.\n\nSecondly, Picsmics, You've given such an opportunity to see a person grow. It's strange to say, but you've basically grown up on this video. Back in your earliest comments, I get the feeling of a young kid, or maybe and early teen, naïve and a bit dumb to the idea of leaving a ton of comments on a video, and through the course of the comments, you can see a young kid slowly turn into a teenager/young adult, seeing aspects of life quickly change and learning a harsh reality we all face in our own ways. The struggles of life didn't manage to stop you though. You STILL found the time to come back and relive some passionate memories, and you know? That is what life is all about I think. You didn't let anything hold you back from coming here and staying consistent. For literally 15 years of your life. Judging by the way you typed all those years ago, that is probably over half your life, and that is REALLY impressive. The dedication to this particular video is actually mindblowing. I am glad to see your situation is becoming better for you, and I hope you're not only doing great, but thriving.\n\nI find it quite strange that I actually found myself caring a lot about you as a person after seeing just some random video about you. But I feel like I took a journey through your life and can't help but hope you find the absolute greatest for yourself! I'll keep coming back and rooting for you when I find the time.\n\nOh, and another one for Drazen if you made it this far, props to you for sticking around through Picsmics' entire journey too. A while back when I started uploading Minecraft videos, I had a dude who'd come in and just leave like 5 comments a week on my videos. Instead of just leaving them, I started deleting them because it felt like spam to me. Seeing another side of this puts my actions in a whole new perspective to me tbh. I kinda wonder what would have happened had I not deleted that kids' comments. Maybe he'd be the next picsmics lmfao!",
    "time": "2 years ago"
  },
